r18-fixtures-judge-2 run 2 (session 19, 2026-10-09; kept by session 20, 2026-10-13): the judge as it stands, over
r18-edits/tgc-2.pairs as then built (kept as r18-edits/tgc-2-run2.pairs, from r18-edits/build-fixtures-2-run2.py),
rc 1 in each shard (receipts renamed r18-fixtures-judge-2-run2-{0,1,2}.* with their .out and .asserts files). K
passed both functions, and every named row failed under its mutant except three, whose rows sealed under them with
no failing assertion: S03 (ordered-number-then-image), S09 (break-image-outside-finding-after-id) and S10
(image-alt-beginning-with-code-span-across-lines). Cause, found by reading the kernel and measured by
r18-rows-probe.{sh,cases,log,rc}: each row completed its construct on the line right after the one that opened it.
vf_para_label judges a candidate only from the next line of its run on, and on each line reads the candidate's label
before it asks vf_settled whether to drop it, so the first time the candidate that opens the construct is judged is
on the completing line, where the label is already read. A mutant of the settled test can change a result only when
the candidate is dropped on a line between the one that opens the construct and the one that completes it. In the
probe, each row as written is refused on the repaired kernel and under all three mutants. With one line inside the
construct (alt text continued on a second line) each is refused on the repaired kernel and under the other two
mutants, and seals (rc 0) under exactly its own mutant; marked 17.0.5 and markdown-it 12.3.2 render each corrected
row with the tier as the first visible text after the image (r18-rows-render.{sh,log,rc}). So none of the three
mutants is equivalent, and the rows are corrected rather than the mutants argued: build-fixtures-2.py adds that line
to the three rows in both passes, and the judge itself is unchanged. Run 2 judges the other 23 mutants as recorded
in its logs; run 3 re-judges all 26 on the corrected pairs.
